    body: "The New Carrow village council met Monday and spent most of the session on a proposed water rate increase that would raise the average residential bill by about $4.80 a month beginning in January.\n\nVillage engineer Sam Diaz walked through the reasoning: two of the three wells need casing work, the distribution main under Fourth Street has failed twice in eighteen months, and the village has been drawing on reserves to cover routine repairs since 2022. Council member Trish Okafor asked whether the increase could be phased over two years. Diaz said it could, and that doing so would cost roughly $60,000 more overall in deferred repair escalation. The council took no vote and will consider the measure again October 20.\n\nA sidewalk replacement on Ellery Street was approved 4-1, with council member Rob Lindt voting no on the grounds that the village has not finished the last sidewalk project it started.\n\nPublic comment ran fifty minutes, of which approximately forty-five concerned deer. Eleven residents spoke. No action was taken on deer, and none was proposed, and the council president noted twice that deer management is a state matter."
